+.TH LLAPI_SCAN_FID 3 2026-08-26 "Lustre User API" "Lustre Library Functions"
+.SH NAME
+llapi_scan_fid \- fill a scan record for one FID
+.SH SYNOPSIS
+.nf
+.B #include <lustre/lustreapi.h>
+.PP
+.BI "int llapi_scan_fid(int " mnt_fd ", const char *" mnt_path ,
+.BI "                   const struct lu_fid *" fid ,
+.BI "                   const struct llapi_scan_param *" sp ,
+.BI "                   llapi_scan_cb_t " cb ", void *" data ");"
+.PP
+.fi
+.SH DESCRIPTION
+.B llapi_scan_fid()
+fills a
+.B struct llapi_scan_rec
+for the object named by
+.I fid
+and calls
+.I cb
+once with it.
+.LP
+It is the fourth way into that record, beside
+.BR llapi_scan_namespace (3),
+which walks what exists below a path,
+.BR llapi_scan_device (3),
+which reads what exists on a target, and
+.BR llapi_scan_changelog (3),
+which reads what happened on an MDT. It exists so that a consumer holding a
+FID fills a record the way a walk fills one \(em layout, HSM state, project id
+and MDT index included \(em rather than from a
+.BR stat (2)
+of its own, which can answer only the predicates
+.BR stat (2)
+covers.
+.LP
+.I mnt_fd
+is an open descriptor on the root of a client mount of the filesystem the FID
+belongs to, as
+.BR llapi_root_path_open (3)
+returns. It is the filesystem: the FID is resolved through it, and a directory,
+or the parent of anything else, is opened through it too, at the name
+.BR llapi_fid2path (3)
+answered. The root, rather than any descriptor in the filesystem, because that
+name is relative to it. The opens that gather a project id, HSM state and a
+regular file's MDT index are still made by pathname, through
+.IR mnt_path .
+.LP
+.I mnt_path
+is that mount's path and is required, but it is only the spelling: it is what
+the record's
+.I lfsr_path
+is built from, since
+.BR llapi_fid2path (3)
+answers relative to the filesystem root and a caller needs the whole name. A
+.I mnt_path
+that does not name the mount
+.I mnt_fd
+is open on mislabels the answer. That is
+where this differs from
+.BR llapi_scan_rec_path (3),
+which formats a name for the caller to read rather than opening it. A caller
+filling records for many FIDs opens the mount once and passes the same
+descriptor and path to each call.
+.LP
+The FID is resolved to a pathname with
+.BR llapi_fid2path (3)
+and the record is gathered there, so the caller needs what that needs:
+.B CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH
+unless
+.B llite.*.user_fid2path
+is set.
+.LP
+.I sp
+is the same parameter block the other scanners take, but only
+.IR lfsp_want ,
+.I lfsp_got
+and
+.I lfsp_filter
+mean anything here:
+.IR lfsp_max_depth ,
+.IR lfsp_stats ,
+.I lfsp_search
+and
+.I lfsp_fsname
+describe a traversal there is none of and are ignored, so that one block can
+be shared with a walk.
+.I lfsp_flags
+is accepted for the same reason \(em a block shared with
+.BR llapi_scan_device (3)
+may carry
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_F_INTERNAL
+\(em and every flag it defines is ignored here, there being no traversal to
+stop and no target of its own to read; a bit outside them is still refused
+with
+.BR \-EINVAL .
+.LP
+.I lfsp_filter
+sees what a directory entry would carry:
+.IR lfsr_path ,
+.I lfsr_name
+and the file type in
+.IR lfsr_stx.stx_mode ,
+with
+.I lfsr_parent_fd
+and
+.I lfsr_fd
+set to \-1. It runs after the FID is resolved to a name and the type is read,
+and before the object is opened or anything else is gathered.
+A
+.I lfsp_thread_count
+above 1 is refused rather than ignored, because a caller setting it has
+misunderstood the call. Pass NULL for the defaults.
+.LP
+The record is filled from the live object, and nothing in it is carried in
+from the caller. A consumer merging the result into a record it already holds
+decides for itself which fields it lets this call supply, and so keeps
+the rule that resolution fills what a stream lacks and never restates what it
+has.
+.LP
+The record is valid only for the duration of the call, as it is for the other
+three: a consumer that needs a field afterwards must copy it, including
+anything
+.IR lfsr_path ,
+.IR lfsr_name ,
+.I lfsr_lmm
+or
+.I lfsr_lmv
+points at.
+.I lfsr_parent_fd
+is \-1 for a directory, which is what a walk reports for one above a single
+thread, and both descriptors are closed when the call returns.
+.LP
+A hardlinked object has one pathname per link and the record is gathered at
+the first the MDT lists, as
+.BR llapi_scan_rec_path (3)
+returns it. The caller does not choose which link that is, so a consumer
+restricting by pathname \(em a subtree filter over
+.I lfsr_path
+\(em takes or leaves a file linked both inside and outside its subtree
+according to that order.
+.LP
+When
+.I lfsp_want
+needs the MDT, the call allocates per object: it runs the same parameter
+setup a search does, roughly 70K of buffers for the layout and the directory
+stripe, freed before it returns.
+That is a per-scan cost for the other entry points and a per-object one here.
+A demand mask of
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE
+alone skips that setup.
+The FID lookup is paid either way: a small buffer and one request to the
+MDT per call.
+.SH RETURN VALUES
+.TP
+0
+The record was filled and
+.I cb
+was called \(em or a
+.I lfsp_filter
+skipped the object, in which case no record was delivered. A single call
+cannot tell the two apart, since
+.I lfsp_stats
+is ignored here.
+.TP
+\-ENOENT
+.I fid
+has no pathname: unlinked since the caller learned of it, or an object a
+target keeps for itself. For a consumer asking what is true now, this is the
+answer rather than an error.
+.TP
+\-ESTALE
+The name
+.I fid
+resolved to no longer holds it: it was unlinked and recreated while the
+record was being gathered, so the record would have described a different
+object. Only a
+.I lfsp_want
+naming a field other than
+.B LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE
+can detect this \(em the FID to compare against comes from the gather, and a
+name-only demand mask does none, so such a call describes whatever held the
+name when it was read.
+.TP
+\-EPERM
+The caller may not resolve FIDs.
+.TP
+\-EBADF
+.I mnt_fd
+is negative. Its own errno, not
+.BR EINVAL ,
+because that one also means \(lqthis FID has no name\(rq \(em see
+.BR llapi_scan_rec_path (3),
+which answers the same way.
+.TP
+\-EINVAL
+.IR mnt_path ,
+.I fid
+or
+.I cb
+is NULL, or
+.I sp
+is malformed \(em a bad
+.IR lfsp_size ,
+an unknown flag, or a
+.I lfsp_thread_count
+above 1. It is also what the server answers for a FID with no name at all;
+see
+.BR ERRORS .
+.PP
+A negative errno otherwise. If
+.I cb
+returns non-zero, that value is returned unchanged. A
+.I lfsp_filter
+returning a positive value skips the object; a negative one is returned
+unchanged, and neither reaches
+.IR cb .
+.SH ERRORS
+.TP 15
+.B -ENOENT
+.I fid
+has no pathname.
+.TP 15
+.B -ESTALE
+the name it resolved to holds a different object now. Not detected when
+.I lfsp_want
+names nothing but
+.BR LLAPI_SCAN_TYPE ;
+see above. A directory shard's FID answers this too, where it is detected:
+fid2path names the striped directory the shard belongs to, whose FID is the
+master's. Resolve the master instead. So does an OST object's FID: fid2path
+names the file that owns it, whose FID is on an MDT. The lookup also goes
+through that OST, so it waits while the OST is down. Name the owner instead
+.RB ( lfsr_owner_fid
+from a scan).
+.TP 15
+.B -EPERM
+the caller may not resolve FIDs; see
+.BR llapi_fid2path_at (3).
+.TP 15
+.B -EBADF
+.I mnt_fd
+is negative.
+.TP 15
+.B -EXDEV
+.I mnt_fd
+is not the mount root: the MDT answers only for the root the mount has.
+Under DNE, an MDT other than MDT0000 does not check this and may answer
+.B -ENOENT
+or a path relative to
+.I mnt_fd
+instead, so pass the mount root.
+.TP 15
+.B -EINVAL
+an argument is NULL or out of range, or
+.I sp
+is malformed \(em but also what the server answers for a FID that
+.BR fid_is_namespace_visible ()
+excludes, such as an
+.I O/<seq>/LAST_ID
+of the kind a target scan reports in quantity.
+.BR llapi_fid2path_at (3)
+returns that unchanged, so treat it as
+.I this object has no name
+as well as a bad argument, as
+.BR llapi_scan_rec_path (3)
+documents.
+.TP 15
+.B -ENAMETOOLONG
+the resolved pathname does not fit under
+.IR mnt_path .
+.SH EXAMPLES
+Fill a record for one FID and read its layout:
+.EX
+        static int cb(const struct llapi_scan_rec *rec, void *data)
+        {
+                if (rec->lfsr_valid & LLAPI_SCAN_LAYOUT)
+                        printf("%s: %u bytes of layout\en",
+                               rec->lfsr_path, rec->lfsr_lmmsize);
+                return 0;
+        }
+\&
+        int mnt_fd;
+        int rc;
+\&
+        rc = llapi_root_path_open("/mnt/lustre", &mnt_fd);
+        if (rc == 0)
+                rc = llapi_scan_fid(mnt_fd, "/mnt/lustre", &fid, NULL, cb,
+                                    NULL);
+.EE
+.SH AVAILABILITY
+.B llapi_scan_fid()
+is part of the
+.BR lustre (7)
+user application interface library since release 2.18.0.
+.\" Added in commit v2_17_57-xxx-gxxxxxxxxx
+.SH SEE ALSO
+.BR llapi_fid2path (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_changelog (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_device (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_namespace (3),
+.BR llapi_scan_rec_path (3),
+.BR lustreapi (7)
